<article class="env-theorem" id="0747">
       
      
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0747">Theorem <span data-tag="0747">11.3.3</span>.</a> Let $A$ be a simple finite $k$-algebra. Then $A$ is a matrix algebra over a finite $k$-algebra $K$ which is a skew field. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We may choose a simple submodule $M \subset A$ and then the $k$-algebra $K = \text{End}_ A(M)$ is a skew field, see Lemma <a href="/tag/0746" data-tag="0746">11.3.2</a>. By Lemma <a href="/tag/0745" data-tag="0745">11.3.1</a> we see that $A = \text{End}_ K(M)$. Since $K$ is a skew field and $M$ is finitely generated (since $\dim _ k(M) < \infty $) we see that $M$ is finite free as a left $K$-module. It follows immediately that $A \cong \text{Mat}(n \times n, K^{op})$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>